/**
 * TASK: Solve the N-Queens puzzle with backtracking.
 *
 * Place n queens on an n x n board so that no two share a row, a column, or a diagonal.
 * Because no two queens may share a row, every solution places exactly one queen per row,
 * which means a solution can be written as an int array where index = row and the value at
 * that index = the 0-based column of the queen in that row.
 *
 *  - countSolutions(n) returns how many distinct placements exist. Reflections and
 *    rotations count separately, so countSolutions(4) is 2 and countSolutions(8) is 92.
 *    For n < 1 return 0. countSolutions(1) is 1, and countSolutions(2) and (3) are 0.
 *
 *  - firstSolution(n) returns the LEXICOGRAPHICALLY SMALLEST solution as that array of
 *    columns, or an array of length 0 when there is no solution (including n < 1).
 *    "Lexicographically smallest" is exactly what you get from backtracking row by row
 *    while trying columns in ascending order and stopping at the first complete placement.
 *
 *      firstSolution(4) -> {1, 3, 0, 2}
 *      firstSolution(1) -> {0}
 *      firstSolution(3) -> {}
 *
 * Two queens at (rowA, colA) and (rowB, colB) attack each other diagonally exactly when
 * Math.abs(rowA - rowB) == Math.abs(colA - colB).
 *
 * n is at most 8 in the tests, so a straightforward backtracking search is fast enough --
 * but do prune: abandon a row as soon as the queen you just placed is attacked, instead of
 * building every arrangement and checking it at the end.
